Q . What are the new features that are added to interface to make closer to abstarct


interface Payment {

    // 1. Abstract
    void pay();

    // 2. Default
    default void generateReceipt() {
        System.out.println("Generating receipt");
    }

    // 3. Static
    static void paymentInfo() {
        System.out.println("Payment information");
    }

    // 4. Private
    private void validate() {
        System.out.println("Validating payment");
    }
}



In Java, an interface defines a contract that classes can implement. An interface can contain abstract, default, static, and private methods.

Abstract methods are methods without an implementation. They are implicitly public abstract, and the implementing class is responsible for providing the implementation.

Default methods were introduced in Java 8 and allow an interface to provide a concrete implementation. 
An implementing class can inherit the default implementation or override it. 
They are particularly useful for adding new behavior to existing interfaces without breaking existing implementations.

Static methods belong to the interface itself rather than to implementing objects. 
They are called using the interface name and are not inherited by implementing classes.

Private methods were introduced in Java 9 and are used as internal helper methods within the interface. 
They help encapsulate implementation details and can be used by other methods in the interface, such as default or static methods.

