**Q. Can you code a situation where you would prefer to use an interface over an abstract class?**

> **Yes. A good real-world example is a payment system. 
I would use an abstract class for common state and common implementation, and interfaces for additional capabilities that different payment types can support.**
>

> abstract class Payment {
>
>     protected double amount;
>     protected String transactionId;
>
>     public Payment(double amount, String transactionId) {
>         this.amount = amount;
>         this.transactionId = transactionId;
>     }
>
>     public void processPayment() {
>         System.out.println("Processing payment of " + amount);
>     }
>
>     abstract void makePayment();
> }
>
> interface Refundable {
>     void refund();
> }
>
> interface Auditable {
>     void generateAuditLog();
> }
>
> class CreditCardPayment extends Payment
>         implements Refundable, Auditable {
>
>     public CreditCardPayment(double amount, String transactionId) {
>         super(amount, transactionId);
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     void makePayment() {
>         System.out.println("Processing credit card payment");
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     public void refund() {
>         System.out.println("Refunding credit card payment");
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     public void generateAuditLog() {
>         System.out.println("Generating credit card audit log");
>     }
> }
>
> class UPIPayment extends Payment
>         implements Refundable, Auditable {
>
>     public UPIPayment(double amount, String transactionId) {
>         super(amount, transactionId);
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     void makePayment() {
>         System.out.println("Processing UPI payment");
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     public void refund() {
>         System.out.println("Refunding UPI payment");
>     }
>
>     @Override
>     public void generateAuditLog() {
>         System.out.println("Generating UPI audit log");
>     }
> }

>
> Here, `CreditCardPayment` and `UPIPayment` extend the `Payment` abstract class
because both are types of payment and share common state such as `amount` and `transactionId`, as well as common behavior such as `processPayment()`.
>
> They implement **multiple interfaces**, `Refundable` and `Auditable`, because those interfaces represent separate capabilities or contracts.
>
> This is one of the key advantages of interfaces: **a Java class can extend only one class but can implement multiple interfaces.**
>
> For example, another class that is not a payment could still implement `Auditable`:
>
> ```java
> class Order implements Auditable {
>
>     @Override
>     public void generateAuditLog() {
>         System.out.println("Generating order audit log");
>     }
 }
> ```
>
> So I would use an **abstract class when classes share common state, constructors,
and implementation**, and I would use **interfaces when I want to define capabilities or contracts that can be combined across different classes**.
>
> **Interview summary:**
> **Abstract classes model a common base or "is-a" relationship, while interfaces model 

capabilities or contracts. Since Java supports single class inheritance but multiple interface implementation,
interfaces are useful when a class needs to have multiple independent capabilities.


