**Q. If two interfaces have the same method signature and a class implements both, how is this situation solved?**

> **If two interfaces declare the same abstract method signature, there is no conflict. 
The implementing class provides one implementation, and that single implementation satisfies the contract of both interfaces.**
>
> ```java
> interface Payment {
>     void process();
> }
>
> interface Transaction {
>     void process();
> }
>
> class CreditCardPayment implements Payment, Transaction {
>
>     @Override
>     public void process() {
>         System.out.println("Processing credit card payment");
>     }
> }
> ```
>
> Here, both `Payment` and `Transaction` require:
>
> ```java
> void process();
> ```
>
> `CreditCardPayment` provides one `process()` method, and that implementation satisfies both interfaces.
>
> **The situation becomes different when both interfaces provide conflicting `default` implementations.** 
In that case, Java cannot automatically decide which implementation to use, so the implementing class must override the method and resolve the conflict explicitly.
>
> ```java
> interface Payment {
>     default void process() {
>         System.out.println("Payment processing");
>     }
> }
>
> interface Transaction {
>     default void process() {
>         System.out.println("Transaction processing");
>     }
> }
>
> class CreditCardPayment implements Payment, Transaction {
>
>     @Override
>     public void process() {
>         Payment.super.process();
>         // or Transaction.super.process();
>         System.out.println("Credit card processing");
>     }
> }
> ```
>
> **Interview summary:**
> **If two interfaces have the same abstract method signature, one implementation in the class is sufficient. 
If they have conflicting default implementations, the implementing class must override the method and 
explicitly resolve the conflict, optionally calling a specific interface's default implementation using `InterfaceName.super.method()`.**



